                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the strategy gap and where value is created`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Hibernia is the group's slowest-growing but most cash-generative division — historically <strong>managed for stability, not ambition</strong>. Decompose ROIC into its margin and capital-turn drivers, rank the equipment, installation and service segments by growth-and-return potential, and name why current strategy delivers only 2% growth. The trap: concluding the market is simply mature, or that you only need more capital. The honest diagnosis is that <em>strategy here is an extrapolation of last year</em> — there has been no genuine choice. Quantify the Year-3 gap to the 8%/16% mandate (~€95M of revenue, ~5 ROIC points).`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Decide one strategic direction, and say what you will stop`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Commit to ONE coherent archetype — <strong>Volume/scale</strong> (heat-pump capacity), <strong>Servitization</strong> (recurring service & controls, higher margin, lower capital intensity), or <strong>Premium/differentiation</strong> (integrated low-carbon systems). Each implies a different capital pattern and ROIC path. The cardinal sin is to <em>hedge</em>: fund a bit of all three and win at none. A real commitment also names what Hibernia will <strong>stop</strong> doing to release the capital — straddling is not a strategy. Given the diagnosis, the lower-capital, higher-margin pivot is the most direct route to 16% ROIC.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Design the planning & control system that delivers it`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `This is the heart of the simulation and of Gibbons' research: a direction not embedded in the planning and control system never becomes behaviour. Choose a <strong>focused KPI set</strong> (not a 12-metric scorecard a financial-control parent will ignore), <strong>reweight the incentives</strong> across commercial, finance and operations so each function is rewarded for the new value driver — not legacy volume, margin-variance and utilisation — pick a planning <strong>cadence</strong>, and choose a parent <strong>control style</strong> Boyne will actually use. The classic failure: change the strategy slide but leave the budget and bonuses untouched. <em>Where does this strategy become someone's measured job?</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Allocate the €48M and defend the plan to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Three live cases demand €78M; group gave you <strong>€48M</strong> and will not reverse the cut. Allocate across the services platform (€22M), heat-pump capacity (€34M) and plant automation (€22M) — plus whatever working capital your strategy needs — then defend a Year-1/2/3 ROIC-and-growth path. The test is <strong>strategic fit</strong>: the money must go where the strategy goes. Funding a servitization strategy with a capacity-expansion plan is incoherent. Name what is <em>deferred or killed</em>, and show the board that strategy, control system and capital are mutually consistent.`,

                    r1_lever_label:         `Decompose ROIC: which lever can credibly close the 11%→16% gap here?`,
                    r1_segment_label:       `Rank the segments: where is growth-and-return potential highest?`,
                    r1_root_label:          `Name the single biggest reason current strategy delivers only 2% growth`,
                    r1_gap_label:           `Quantify the Year-3 revenue gap to the 8% mandate (€M)`,
                    r1_gap_hint:            `Trailing 2% vs mandated 8% over three years ≈ €95M of added revenue Hibernia is short.`,
                    lever_both:             `Both margin AND capital turn — mix-shift to higher-margin revenue while releasing/working capital harder`,
                    lever_margin:           `Margin only — push price on the existing equipment book`,
                    lever_capturn:          `Capital turn only — sweat the existing assets, no mix change`,
                    lever_volume:           `Neither — just sell more boxes at today's margins`,
                    seg_service:            `Service & controls — high margin, low capital intensity, recurring`,
                    seg_equipment:          `Equipment — the cash-generative but slow-growing core`,
                    seg_install:            `Installation — high volume, thin margin, working-capital heavy`,
                    root_legacy:            `"Managed for the average" — strategy is an extrapolation of last year, no genuine choice`,
                    root_market:            `The market is simply mature — nothing the division does will change it`,
                    root_capital:           `Not enough capital — growth needs more capex than group will give`,

