                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_lever_both:    `<strong>Right — both levers.</strong> Closing 11%→16% needs margin (mix-shift toward higher-margin revenue) AND capital turn (releasing working capital, automation). Pulling only one rarely covers five points, and the chosen lever must match the strategy you pick next round.`,
                    coach_r1_lever_volume:  `<strong>That's the "manage for the average" reflex.</strong> Selling more boxes at today's margins barely moves ROIC and burns working capital. The gap is a mix-and-efficiency problem, not a volume problem.`,
                    coach_r1_seg_equipment: `<strong>Equipment is the cash engine, but it's the slow-growing core.</strong> It funds the future; it isn't where the 8%/16% gap gets closed. Look at where margin and capital intensity actually favour you.`,
                    coach_r1_seg_install:   `<strong>Installation is a trap for this mandate.</strong> High volume, thin margin and working-capital heavy — it depresses both sides of the ROIC equation. It is the segment to shrink, not lead with.`,
                    coach_r1_root_legacy:   `<strong>That's the honest root cause.</strong> Strategy here has been an extrapolation of last year — no genuine choice, no stop-doing list. Naming that is what lets Round 2 be a real decision rather than another budget.`,
                    coach_r1_root_market:   `<strong>"The market is mature" is the comfortable answer.</strong> The same market has higher-margin service and premium niches your competitors are taking. The constraint is the division's own habit of not choosing, not the market.`,
                    coach_r1_root_capital:  `<strong>More capital won't fix a strategy problem.</strong> Group is cutting capital 20%, not adding it. The gap closes through a sharper choice and a better capital pattern — not a bigger cheque.`,
                    coach_r1_gap_low:       `<strong>That under-states the gap.</strong> Trailing 2% against a mandated 8% over three years is roughly €95M of missing revenue and about 5 ROIC points. Size it honestly or you'll under-resource the response.`,
                    coach_r2_servitization: `<strong>Servitization fits the diagnosis cleanly.</strong> Higher-margin recurring revenue with lower capital intensity drives ROIC from both directions — the most direct route to 16%. The cost is a slower ramp and new capabilities, so commit and back it.`,
                    coach_r2_premium:       `<strong>Premium can work if you truly commit.</strong> Integrated low-carbon systems lift margin and differentiate, but demand brand, capability and discipline. It is a coherent bet — just don't dilute it next round.`,
                    coach_r2_volume:        `<strong>Volume/scale is the lowest-ROIC route.</strong> Heat-pump capacity grows revenue but is capital-hungry, so it strains the very ratio group is squeezing. Defensible only if your capital pattern and control system make it genuinely efficient.`,
                    coach_r2_hedge:         `<strong>You've hedged across all three.</strong> With €48M against €78M of demand you've under-resourced everything and will win at none — ROIC stalls near 12%. Which one are you actually betting the division on?`,
                    coach_r2_stopnothing:   `<strong>A strategy with no stop-doing list is a wish.</strong> If you cut nothing, you can't fund the bet within €48M and your best people stay spread across everything. Commitment means deciding what to exit.`,
                    coach_r2_lowconviction: `<strong>Low conviction reads as hedging by another name.</strong> A direction you only half-commit to won't reorganise the division behind it. Either back this bet firmly or you'll drift back to the average.`,
                    coach_r3_kpi_focused:   `<strong>A focused 4–6 KPI set is right.</strong> ROIC, growth, recurring-revenue mix and two strategy-specific drivers give the parent numbers it will actually use, and the organisation a scoreboard it can see itself in.`,
                    coach_r3_kpi_inflated:  `<strong>A 12-metric scorecard will be ignored.</strong> Boyne runs a financial-control model — hand them fourteen metrics and they'll squeeze the two financial ones and override the rest. Design the control system the parent will actually use.`,
                    coach_r3_kpi_financial: `<strong>Two pure-financial KPIs are parent-friendly but strategy-blind.</strong> ROIC and cash alone won't tell anyone whether the new strategy is taking hold. Keep them, but add the few drivers that make the direction visible.`,
                    coach_r3_realign:       `<strong>This is where strategy becomes behaviour.</strong> Retiring the legacy volume/margin/utilisation bonuses and rewarding the new value driver across all three functions is exactly Gibbons' point — the strategy is now someone's measured, paid job, not a slide.`,
                    coach_r3_addgrowth:     `<strong>A growth bonus on top of the old ones pulls people two ways.</strong> Commercial still chases volume because that still pays; the new strategy competes with the legacy incentive instead of replacing it. Realign, don't layer.`,
                    coach_r3_legacy:        `<strong>You've changed the slide, not the system.</strong> Commercial is bonused on volume, plant on utilisation, finance on margin variance — leave that and the organisation keeps optimising for the old goals. Where does this strategy become someone's measured job?`,
                    coach_r3_annual:        `<strong>The legacy annual budget locks you to last year.</strong> A new strategy needs a rolling forecast and milestone reviews so you can re-plan as it unfolds, not discover at year-end that you optimised the wrong plan.`,
                    coach_r3_style_planning: `<strong>Asking a financial-control parent to co-own operating detail will be rejected.</strong> Boyne wants ROIC and cash, not deep involvement. Negotiate strategic control — a few numbers they'll use plus milestones — rather than a style they won't run.`,
                    coach_r4_aligned:       `<strong>Strategic fit achieved.</strong> The capital is concentrated where your stated strategy goes, you stayed inside €48M, and the ROIC bridge is walkable. That is a plan whose words and numbers agree — a credible board case.`,
                    coach_r4_spread:        `<strong>You've spread the capital across all three cases.</strong> Thin slices of €48M fund nothing decisively and contradict a committed strategy — the numbers reveal a hedge even if the words claimed a choice. Concentrate behind the bet.`,
                    coach_r4_misaligned:    `<strong>The money didn't follow the strategy.</strong> You committed to one direction but funded another case — that's a strategic-fit failure. Funding servitization with a capacity plan (or vice versa) is incoherent; align the capital with the words.`,
                    coach_r4_wishlist:      `<strong>You've funded €78M as if the cut never happened.</strong> Group gave you €48M and will not reverse it — you're €30M over, so you haven't allocated, you've wish-listed. What gets deferred, and what's the ROIC cost of deferring it?`,
                    coach_r4_words:         `<strong>The words and the numbers disagree.</strong> If the capital went somewhere other than the stated strategy, the board reads the spreadsheet, not the slide. A plan whose money contradicts its strategy is not credible.`,
                    coach_r4_defhedged:     `<strong>"Keeping flexibility" across all three is hedging.</strong> Flexibility sounds prudent but with €48M it means under-funding the actual bet. The board wants to see conviction backed by capital, not optionality that wins nowhere.`,
                    coach_r4_boardready:    `<strong>That's a board-ready plan.</strong> Aligned capital, an explicitly deferred case with its ROIC cost named, and a defence where strategy, control system and money all tell the same story. This is a plan that survives the December review.`,

